from common import lesson, lab, cite, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(0,0,'orientation','An activation is a vector',
 'Every question this course answers is a question about vectors: where one points, how long it is, how much of it lies along a direction, and how a matrix moves it.',
 '''<h2 id="the-object">The object you will be studying</h2>
<p>Run GPT-2 small on a sentence and stop it after the seventh transformer block. For each token it has produced a list of 768 numbers. That list is an <strong>activation</strong>, and the whole course is about what you can legitimately say about such lists. An interpretability claim of the form “this direction represents digits” is, concretely, a claim that one particular list of 768 numbers, dotted with the activation of each token, gives a larger number on digit tokens than on others. You cannot evaluate that claim without the dot product, and you cannot say what it would mean for the claim to be wrong without projections, bases, and a held-out check. Those are the contents of this course.</p>
<p>A <strong>vector</strong> is an ordered list of numbers of fixed length. The length of the list is the number of dimensions. Two numbers make a point in the plane; three make a point in space; 768 make a point in a space you cannot draw but can compute in exactly the same way. Every rule you learn on two dimensions, where you can check it with a pencil, carries over unchanged to 768. That is the pedagogical bargain of this course: each idea is shown first on a two-dimensional example you can verify by hand, then on the real 768-dimensional activations.</p>
<div class="diagram">token → activation vector x ∈ ℝ⁷⁶⁸ → x · d (a scalar per token) → does it track a label?</div>
<h2 id="fixture">The fixture: real activations, nothing simulated</h2>
<p>The course ships one layer of GPT-2 small’s residual stream for 302 tokens of an original passage about a fictional harbour town, as <code>labs/data/activations.npy</code> with shape (302, 768). It also ships the four weight matrices of one attention head, so that the matrix lessons act on real maps rather than made-up numbers. The labels you will test directions against (“is this token a digit”, “does it follow a full stop”) were computed from the token strings, never from the activations. <code>labs/data/README.md</code> records how the file was produced and the two surprises it contains. Every number printed in these lessons was computed from that file; the transcript is in <code>research/verify/lab-transcript.txt</code>.</p>
<p>Why one layer? Because the moves do not depend on the layer. Why GPT-2 small? Because it runs on a laptop and is the model most of the published interpretability work you will read was done on. The sibling <a href="../../../python/python-tut/index.html">Python course</a> shows how activations like these are cached with hooks; here they are simply loaded with numpy.</p>
<h2 id="shapes">Shapes as a type system</h2>
<p>If you write Elixir or Rust you are used to types that are checked before anything runs. Treat array shapes the same way. An activation has shape (768,). A batch of activations has shape (302, 768): 302 rows, one per token, each a 768-vector. A weight matrix that maps the 768-dimensional residual stream into a 64-dimensional head space has shape (768, 64). Before you compute anything, write the shapes and check that they compose; a shape error is a type error, and the lessons will keep writing shapes as comments next to code for that reason.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py inspect</code></pre>
'''+out('''activations: shape (302, 768), dtype on disk float16, loaded as float32
tokens: 302; first eight: ['The', ' harbour', ' town', ' of', ' Weston', ' Hollow', ' kept', ' careful']
labels: is_digit (45 ones), after_period (23 ones), starts_with_space (241 ones), capitalised (29 ones)
token 12 = '42': first six of 768 coordinates [-2.182 -2.564  1.455  1.724 -1.904 -3.473]
  largest |coordinate| at index 447, value 41.81
head weights: W_Q (768, 64), W_K (768, 64), W_V (768, 64), W_O (64, 768)''')+'''
<p>Token 12 is the string <code>'42'</code>, the second half of the year 1842, which the tokenizer split as <code>' 18'</code> and <code>'42'</code>. Its activation begins −2.182, −2.564, 1.455, … and its largest coordinate, 41.81, sits at index 447. Keep that index in mind; it returns in lesson 06, where you will see that a large coordinate in the neuron basis is a fact about the basis as much as about the token.</p>
<h2 id="map">The route through the course</h2>
<p>Module 0 treats single vectors: adding and scaling them (lesson 01), measuring length and angle (02), and the central move of projecting onto a direction (03). Module 1 treats matrices as maps: shapes (04), multiplication as composition and as batching (05), bases (06), and rank (07). Module 2 removes a direction cleanly (08) and fits a direction to labels (09). Module 3 reads the spectrum of a matrix: eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix (10), PCA on the activations (11), and the SVD of a weight product (12). Module 4 adds the calculus minimum (13) and the question that decides whether any of this was real (14), then the readiness review (15).</p>
<p>There are no proofs. There are checks: numerical identities you can run, such as that a residual is orthogonal to its direction or that the squared norms of a projection and its residual add up to the squared norm of the original. When a lesson states a rule, the lab prints the check. If you want the proofs later, the <a href="../appendices/d-reading-map.html">reading map</a> points to the 3Blue1Brown chapter for each lesson, which supplies the geometric argument.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>“The model represents X along direction d” is a sentence about a dot product. Before accepting it, you will want to know the scale of d, what the projection looks like on tokens that were not used to find d, and what a random direction would have scored. By lesson 14 you will be able to ask all three.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Treating the 768 coordinates as 768 meaningful quantities. They are coordinates in one basis, chosen by how the weights happen to be stored. Lesson 06 shows the same activation written in a different basis with entirely different numbers and the same length.</p></div>''',
 'A colleague says “the activation for the token 42 has a big value in dimension 447, so dimension 447 must be doing something about numbers.” What two things would you want to check before agreeing?',
 'First, whether dimension 447 is large for every token, not only numbers; the inspect output cannot tell you, but lesson 02 shows the same index dominates the fixture’s mean. Second, whether “dimension 447” is a direction with any privileged meaning at all: it is one axis of the neuron basis, and lesson 06 shows that the residual stream can be rotated into another basis without changing the model. A claim about numbers needs a direction found from the number label and tested on tokens not used to find it.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py --help','python3 labs/linalg_lab.py inspect --token 0'],
 'List the lab commands, then inspect token 0. Compare its largest coordinate with token 12’s.',
 'Token 0 is the word “The” at position 0, and its largest coordinate is enormous compared with token 12’s 41.81. Lesson 02 measures it: the first token’s vector is about thirty-six times longer than a typical token’s. Most later commands exclude position 0 and say so.'), ['framework','arena']),

lesson(1,0,'vectors-and-sums','Vectors, addition, and scaling',
 'Add two activations coordinate by coordinate, scale one by a number, and you have everything a residual stream does between layers.',
 '''<h2 id="two-dims">Two dimensions first</h2>
<p>Take u = (3, 1) and v = (1, 2). Their sum is (3 + 1, 1 + 2) = (4, 3): add matching coordinates. Scaling u by 2 gives (6, 2): multiply every coordinate. Subtracting is adding the negative: u − v = (2, −1). Draw u and v as arrows from the origin and the sum is the far corner of the parallelogram they make; scaling stretches an arrow along its own line; a negative scale flips it. Nothing else is allowed. There is no multiplication of two vectors into a vector, and there is no division.</p>
<div class="math">u + v = (u₁ + v₁, …, u_d + v_d)<br>c·u = (c·u₁, …, c·u_d)<br>2u − 3v is a <em>linear combination</em> of u and v</div>
<p>A <strong>linear combination</strong> is a sum of scaled vectors. Every vector you will meet in this course that is not raw data is a linear combination of something: a direction is a combination of basis vectors, a projection is a scalar times a direction, a residual-stream activation is a combination of layer outputs. The two operations, addition and scaling, are the whole of what “linear” means, and lesson 04 defines a linear map as a function that respects exactly them.</p>
<h2 id="residual">The residual stream is a sum</h2>
<p>In a transformer each block reads the current activation, computes something, and adds its result back. After block 6, the activation for a token is the token embedding plus the position embedding plus the output of each of the seven attention layers and seven MLP layers before it. The authors of the transformer-circuits framework put it directly: “The residual stream is simply the sum of the output of all the previous layers and the original embedding.” '''+cite('framework')+''' That sentence is why vector addition is the first lesson. The 768 numbers in the fixture are a sum of fifteen other 768-vectors, and much of interpretability consists of asking which of the summands is responsible for which part of the total.</p>
<p>The fixture holds only the total, so the lab demonstrates addition with what it has: two tokens’ activations, their sum, a scaled copy, their difference, and the difference between one token and the mean of all tokens. Each is a legitimate vector in the same 768-dimensional space, and the last one, the centred activation, is the object PCA will work on in lesson 11.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py vectors</code></pre>
'''+out('''u = token 12 '42', v = token 40 ' boats'
u[:5]       = [-2.182 -2.564  1.455  1.724 -1.904]
v[:5]       = [ 5.984  1.1   -4.691  1.996  1.895]
(u+v)[:5]   = [ 3.803 -1.465 -3.236  3.72  -0.01 ]
(2u)[:5]    = [-4.363 -5.129  2.91   3.447 -3.809]
(u-v)[:5]   = [-8.166 -3.664  6.146 -0.272 -3.799]
mean of tokens 1..301, first five coordinates: [ 0.317  0.162 -0.401  0.064  0.22 ]
u - mean, first five: [-2.498 -2.726  1.856  1.66  -2.125]
check: (u+v)-v == u exactly in exact arithmetic; here max |((u+v)-v) - u| = 0.00e+00''')+'''
<p>Check the first coordinate by hand: −2.182 + 5.984 = 3.802, printed as 3.803 because the stored values carry more digits than shown. That is the entire operation, repeated 768 times. The last line is a reminder that the fixture is stored in float16 and loaded as float32: addition of these particular values happened to be exact, but in general floating-point sums are not, and when a check prints 10⁻¹³ rather than 0 that is rounding, not a counterexample.</p>
<h2 id="difference-vectors">Difference vectors are directions</h2>
<p>The difference u − v points from v to u. If u is a digit token and v is not, the difference is a first, crude guess at a “digit direction”: it is the vector you would add to v to arrive at u. Averaging many such differences gives the mean-difference direction used in lesson 03, which is simply the mean activation of the digit tokens minus the mean activation of the others, itself a linear combination of all 301 activations with coefficients +1/45 and −1/256.</p>
<p>Subtracting the mean from every token is the same idea applied to the whole batch. Coordinates that are large for every token, such as index 447 from lesson 00, are large in the mean, and subtracting the mean removes what every token shares. What remains is where tokens differ, and only differences can track a label. This is why the covariance and PCA lessons begin by centring.</p>
<h2 id="shapes-again">Shapes</h2>
<p>Addition needs equal shapes: (768,) + (768,) is allowed, (768,) + (64,) is a type error. Numpy additionally lets you add a (768,) vector to every row of a (302, 768) matrix, which is how <code>X − X.mean(axis=0)</code> centres a whole batch in one line; the rule is called broadcasting, and the shape of the result is (302, 768). When a broadcast surprises you, write the shapes out before trusting the numbers.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Steering a model by adding a scaled vector to its residual stream is vector addition and scaling and nothing else: x′ = x + c·d. The interesting questions are which d, which c, and what changes downstream. The arithmetic is this lesson.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading “the model adds a feature” as if features were ingredients that keep their identity in the sum. Once two vectors are added you cannot recover either from the total alone. Separating them again is the subject of lessons 03, 08 and 11, and it needs extra information: a direction, a label, or a variance criterion.</p></div>''',
 'The mean activation of the 301 tokens after position 0 has first coordinate 0.317 and token 12’s is −2.182. If you add the mean-centred activation of token 12 to the mean-centred activation of token 40, and then add the mean back, what do you get, and why does it matter?',
 'You get (u − m) + (v − m) + m = u + v − m, which is not u + v. Centring is linear but not idempotent under addition: summing centred vectors double-subtracts the mean. In practice this means you must decide once whether you are working with raw or centred activations and keep every vector in the same convention; mixing the two produces a vector shifted by a multiple of the mean, and the mean is large in exactly the coordinates that dominate norms.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py vectors --token 1 --other 203'],
 'Tokens 1 and 203 are both the word “harbour” in different sentences. Compute their difference and compare its first coordinates with the difference between tokens 12 and 40 above.',
 'The two “harbour” activations are not identical; context after block 6 has changed them, so the difference is not zero. But its coordinates are smaller than those of the difference between a number and a noun. Lesson 02 turns that impression into a number with the cosine similarity.'), ['framework','3b1b']),

lesson(2,0,'dot-product-and-similarity','Dot product, norm, and cosine similarity',
 'The dot product is the one operation that turns two vectors into a number. Length and angle are both built from it, and “similar activations” means one of them.',
 '''<h2 id="dot">One operation, three readings</h2>
<p>The <strong>dot product</strong> of u and v multiplies matching coordinates and adds the results: u · v = u₁v₁ + u₂v₂ + … + u_dv_d. For u = (3, 1) and v = (1, 2) that is 3 + 2 = 5. It is a single number. It is symmetric, u · v = v · u, and linear in each argument: (2u) · v = 2(u · v) and (u + w) · v = u · v + w · v. Those two properties are why it survives into 768 dimensions without changing character.</p>
<div class="math">u · v = Σᵢ uᵢ vᵢ<br>‖u‖ = √(u · u)   (the norm, or length)<br>cos θ = u · v / (‖u‖ ‖v‖)   (cosine similarity, between −1 and 1)</div>
<p>The <strong>norm</strong> of u is the square root of u · u. For (3, 4) that is √(9 + 16) = 5, the Pythagorean length, and the formula is the same for 768 coordinates. The geometric fact that makes the dot product useful is u · v = ‖u‖ ‖v‖ cos θ, where θ is the angle between the arrows. So the dot product is large when the vectors are long <em>and</em> aligned, zero when they are perpendicular, negative when they point against each other. Dividing by both norms removes length and leaves the cosine of the angle: the <strong>cosine similarity</strong>. The 3Blue1Brown chapter on dot products shows the geometry; this lesson uses it.</p>
<h2 id="which">Which one is “similarity”?</h2>
<p>When someone says two activations are similar they might mean a large dot product or a large cosine. The two disagree whenever norms differ, and in this fixture norms differ by a factor of thirty-six. Ask the lab for the norms and for the tokens most similar to token 12.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py similar</code></pre>
'''+out('''norms: token 0 'The' 3047.1; median over all tokens 85.1; min 66.6 at token 191 '\\n'
query token 12 '42', norm 88.5
most similar by cosine (excluding itself):
  token  29 '71'           cos 0.857  dot     6928.7  norm 91.4
  token 274 '42'           cos 0.799  dot     6252.8  norm 88.4
  token  70 ' 1896'        cos 0.753  dot     6204.6  norm 93.0
  token 111 ' 1903'        cos 0.747  dot     6544.2  norm 98.9
  token  11 ' 18'          cos 0.733  dot     5608.2  norm 86.5
least similar:
  token 229 ' every'       cos 0.314  dot     2353.4  norm 84.6
  token 207 ' working'     cos 0.302  dot     2245.8  norm 84.1
  token 147 ' a'           cos 0.239  dot     1879.5  norm 89.0
cosine to every other token: mean 0.460, min 0.239, max 0.857''')+'''
<p>Three things to read. First, the tokens nearest to <code>'42'</code> by cosine are <code>'71'</code> (the second half of 1871), the other <code>'42'</code> later in the passage, and three year tokens. The activation geometry after block 6 groups number pieces together, which is what a “digit direction” will exploit in the next lesson. Second, the ranking by dot product is not the same as the ranking by cosine: <code>' 1903'</code> has a larger dot product than <code>' 1896'</code> but a smaller cosine, because its norm is larger. Third, no cosine is near zero. The mean cosine between token 12 and every other token is 0.460, and the least similar token, <code>' a'</code>, still has cosine 0.239. Activations in this layer all share a large common component, the mean you met in lesson 01, so raw cosines are compressed into a band well above zero. If you want “unrelated” to read as zero you must centre first.</p>
<h2 id="outlier">The first token</h2>
<p>Token 0 has norm 3,047, the median token 85. This is an observed property of the fixture (the README records it), and it is the first thing a norm computation would have told you about this data. It matters operationally: any dot product with token 0 is about thirty-six times larger than the same angle would produce with another token; any average over tokens that includes position 0 is dominated by it; and PCA on the raw matrix, in lesson 11, spends its first component almost entirely on this one row. The lab excludes position 0 from most commands and says so each time. The lesson is not “GPT-2 is odd”, it is “compute norms before you compute anything that sums over tokens”.</p>
<h2 id="hand">A worked two-dimensional check</h2>
<p>u = (3, 4), v = (4, −3). Then u · v = 12 − 12 = 0, so they are perpendicular; cosine 0 without computing any norm. Now v = (6, 8) = 2u: u · v = 18 + 32 = 50, ‖u‖ = 5, ‖v‖ = 10, cosine 50/50 = 1. Same direction, different length, cosine exactly one. Finally v = (−3, −4): cosine −1. Three cases, three readings of the same formula; the browser <a href="../appendices/b-explorers.html">projection explorer</a> lets you drag u and v and watch the cosine move.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Nearest-neighbour searches over activations, similarity between a probe direction and a neuron, and the attention score between a query and a key are all dot products or cosines. Decide, and state, which one you are using and whether you centred. A nearest-neighbour list computed on uncentred vectors with one giant row is a list of tokens that most resemble the giant row.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reporting a cosine similarity of 0.46 as “moderate similarity”. In this layer 0.46 is the mean cosine between a number token and an arbitrary token. What counts as high is relative to that background, which you measure, not assume. Lesson 14 turns this into a baseline.</p></div>''',
 'Two directions d₁ and d₂ have cosine 0.9. Someone projects every activation onto each and reports that the two projections correlate at 0.9 across tokens. Is that a finding?',
 'Not by itself. If d₁ and d₂ are nearly the same direction, the projections onto them are nearly the same numbers for every input, so a high correlation between them is guaranteed by the geometry of the directions and says nothing about the data. A finding would be two directions with low cosine whose projections nevertheless track each other, or one direction whose projection tracks an external label (lesson 03) on held-out tokens (lesson 14).',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py similar --token 0'],
 'Query with token 0, the outlier. Read the dot products and the cosines.',
 'The dot products are in the hundreds of thousands, because every one includes the factor 3,047. The cosines are still ordinary numbers between −1 and 1; dividing by the norms removed the scale. Whether the cosine ranking for token 0 means anything is another matter: a vector thirty-six times longer than its neighbours may be doing something other than representing the word “The”, and this course makes no claim about what.'), ['3b1b','framework']),

lesson(3,0,'projection-onto-a-direction','Projection onto a direction: the central move',
 'Projecting every activation onto one candidate direction turns a (302, 768) matrix into 302 numbers. Most of interpretability is deciding whether those numbers mean something.',
 '''<h2 id="definition">What a projection is</h2>
<p>Take a vector v and a nonzero direction d. Write d̂ = d / ‖d‖ for the unit vector along d. The <strong>projection</strong> of v onto the line through d has a coefficient and a vector:</p>
<div class="math">coefficient  c = v · d̂ = v · d / ‖d‖   (a scalar; signed length along d)<br>projection  p = c · d̂   (a vector on the line through d)<br>residual  r = v − p   (a vector, with r · d = 0)</div>
<p>In two dimensions, v = (3, 4) onto d = (1, 0): d̂ = (1, 0), c = 3, p = (3, 0), r = (0, 4). Onto d = (1, 1): d̂ = (1, 1)/√2, c = 7/√2 ≈ 4.95, p = (3.5, 3.5), r = (−0.5, 0.5). Check that r · d = −0.5 + 0.5 = 0 in the second case: the residual is always perpendicular to the direction, because the projection took exactly the component along d and nothing else. Check also that ‖p‖² + ‖r‖² = 24.5 + 0.5 = 25 = ‖v‖²: Pythagoras, since p and r are perpendicular. These two identities are the tests in <code>labs/test_linalg.py</code> and the checks the lab prints.</p>
<p>The coefficient does not depend on the length of d, because d̂ does not: projecting onto (10, 10) gives the same c, p and r as projecting onto (1, 1). The dot product v · d, by contrast, scales with ‖d‖. When a paper reports “the projection of activations onto the feature direction”, it matters whether they normalised d; if they did not, the numbers are v · d and are only comparable between tokens, not between directions.</p>
<h2 id="per-token">A scalar per token</h2>
<p>Apply the coefficient formula to every row of the activation matrix at once: X d̂ has shape (302, 768) times (768,) giving (302,), one number per token. This is the central move of the course and of a great deal of interpretability practice: a candidate direction, a batch of activations, a scalar per token, and then the question of whether the scalars line up with something you can name.</p>
<p>The simplest candidate direction for a binary label is the mean-difference direction from lesson 01: the mean activation of tokens with the label minus the mean activation of tokens without it. The lab builds it for the digit label and projects every token (position 0 excluded).</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py project --label is_digit</code></pre>
'''+out('''direction d = mean(is_digit=1) - mean(is_digit=0); |d| = 35.50
projection coefficient x.d/|d| for every token: shape (301,)
  mean over is_digit=1 tokens:    25.68   mean over is_digit=0 tokens:    -9.82
highest coefficients:
     33.70  token 123 ' 215'       label 1
     33.25  token 100 ' 12'        label 1
     32.33  token  36 '02'         label 1
     32.05  token  39 ' 63'        label 1
     31.57  token 113 ' 48'        label 1
     31.13  token  90 ' 640'       label 1
lowest coefficients:
    -18.01  token 203 ' harbour'   label 0
    -18.13  token   1 ' harbour'   label 0
    -18.40  token 246 ' museum'    label 0
    -18.70  token 195 ' town'      label 0
token 12 '42': coefficient 19.71, |projection| 19.71, |residual| 86.29, |x| 88.51
  check |proj|^2 + |resid|^2 = 7833.8 vs |x|^2 = 7833.8; resid.d = -1.99e-13''')+'''
<p>Read it top to bottom. The direction has norm 35.5, so the gap between the class means along d̂ is 35.5 by construction: the mean coefficient of digit tokens is 25.68 and of the others −9.82, and 25.68 − (−9.82) = 35.5. That is not evidence of anything; it is what the mean difference does. The evidence, such as it is, lies in the ordering: the six highest coefficients are all number tokens, the lowest are all nouns, and token 12 sits at 19.71, on the digit side. The last two lines are the Pythagoras and orthogonality checks on real data: 19.71² + 86.29² = 7,833.8 = 88.51², and r · d is −2·10⁻¹³, zero up to rounding.</p>
<p>Notice the sizes. The coefficient along d is 19.71 while the residual has length 86.29. The digit direction accounts for a small part of token 12’s activation; most of the vector is elsewhere. “This token projects strongly onto the digit direction” is a statement relative to other tokens, not a statement that the token is mostly digit-ness.</p>
<h2 id="not-yet">What this does not establish</h2>
<p>The projection was computed on the same tokens used to build the direction. A mean-difference direction will always separate the means of the two groups it was built from; the question of whether it separates <em>new</em> digit tokens from new non-digit tokens is lesson 09 and lesson 14. And the label was chosen by you. The projection can confirm or refute “d tracks is_digit”; it cannot tell you that d tracks nothing else. The lab’s <code>correlation</code> command, later, projects the same direction against the other labels for that reason.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Almost every “feature” you will read about, from a probe direction to a sparse-autoencoder decoder column, is used by projecting activations onto it and reading the scalars. When you see a plot of “feature activation” over tokens, you are looking at c = x · d̂ (or x · d) per token, possibly thresholded. Ask how d was chosen, whether it was normalised, and which tokens were held out.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Comparing projection coefficients across two directions with different norms without normalising. x · d doubles if d doubles. The coefficient x · d̂ does not.</p></div>''',
 'The mean coefficient of digit tokens along d̂ is 25.68 and of non-digit tokens −9.82; the gap equals ‖d‖ = 35.5. Would a random label with 45 ones produce a gap of zero?',
 'No. Build the mean-difference direction for any label and the gap between the class means along it equals ‖d‖ by construction, which is positive unless the two means coincide exactly. With 45 random tokens against 256 in 768 dimensions the sample means differ by chance, so ‖d‖ is positive and the gap is positive. The gap is therefore not a measure of whether the label is represented. What distinguishes a real label is that the direction built on some tokens separates other tokens, and that the gap is large relative to the spread within each class. Lesson 14 computes both.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py project --label after_period --token 100'],
 'Build the mean-difference direction for “token follows a full stop” and project. Look at which tokens score highest and at token 100 (<code>&#39; 12&#39;</code>, a digit, not after a period).',
 'The highest coefficients should be sentence-initial words, and the ordering is less clean than for digits: after_period has only 23 positive tokens, so the direction is estimated from fewer examples. Token 100 should be on the negative side. Compare its coefficient with the gap ‖d‖ printed on the first line to judge how far from the boundary it sits.'), ['3b1b','arena']),
]
